INT. HOTEL LAUNDRY ROOM - DAY

Steam, sheets, rolling carts. A brass bell on the counter.

INSPECTOR WALTER PRYCE, 50s, rumpled trench coat, hat in hand, leans on the counter. Behind it, MRS. ADELAIDE VOSS, 60s, laundress, folds pillowcases in perfect squares without looking down.

PRYCE
Room two-fourteen sent down a bundle Tuesday.

MRS. VOSS
Many rooms send bundles.

PRYCE
This one had a monogram. "H.L."

MRS. VOSS
There are two Hs in the hotel. One is a duchess.

PRYCE
And the other?

MRS. VOSS
Left Wednesday.

The door swings and a young bellhop, FINNEGAN "FINN" DOYLE, 17, buttons crooked, hurries in with a cart.

FINN
Mrs. Voss, the Hargrove suite wants--

He sees Pryce and stops.

PRYCE
Hargrove, you say?

FINN
I didn't say anything.

^MRS. VOSS
Finn, the cart.

Pryce smiles slowly and taps his hat against his palm.

PRYCE
Mrs. Voss, I'd like to see that bundle.

MRS. VOSS
It's been laundered.

PRYCE
I thought it might have been.
